        <div id="protein" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Which Has More Protein per Scoop?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Isopure Zero Carb has more protein per scoop and per gram of powder: 25 g in a 31 g scoop, against 20 g in a 27 g scoop for Clean Simple Eats, per the labels. That is about 81% protein by weight for Isopure and about 74% for Clean Simple Eats.
          </p>

          <p>
            Unlike most head-to-heads, the scoop sizes here are not a trick of measurement. Clean Simple Eats has simply chosen a smaller serving, sized at 100 calories, which suits people who track food in round numbers. The catch is that one scoop is 5 g short of the 25 g many lifters aim for after training. To match a single Isopure scoop you need 1.25 scoops of Clean Simple Eats, about 34 g of powder and about 125 calories, against Isopure's 110.
          </p>

          <p>
            The density gap comes from what sits next to the whey. Clean Simple Eats adds cocoa, coconut milk powder, sea salt and an enzyme blend to its isolate. Isopure adds a vitamin and mineral blend, lecithin, flavor, salt and a gum. Both lists are short, but the Isopure scoop is simply more protein, which is why protein density is the largest part of our <a href='/methodology/'>Label Score</a> and the biggest single reason Isopure scores higher.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you split your protein across the day in smaller doses, the 20 g scoop is not a drawback. A 20 g serving in a smoothie or oatmeal is plenty for most people, and the lower calorie count makes it easier to fit into a snack. It only becomes a cost issue when you scale it up, which the next section covers.
          </p>
        </div>

