        <div id="testing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Is Clean Simple Eats or Isopure Third-Party Tested?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Neither has a named sport certification. Clean Simple Eats says its products are third-party tested, and Isopure says its powders are tested by its own quality lab, but we found no <a href='https://www.nsfsport.com/' rel='nofollow noopener' target='_blank'>NSF Certified for Sport</a> or Informed Choice listing for either brand's whey at the time of writing. An NSF Certified for Sport search for Isopure returned no results when we checked on 20 November 2026.
          </p>

          <p>
            The two claims are worth slightly different amounts. Clean Simple Eats describes outside testing for pathogens, heavy metals and gluten, as covered in our <a href='/clean-simple-eats-protein-powder-review/'>Clean Simple Eats review</a>, but it names no lab and publishes no lot certificates we could find. Isopure, per our <a href='/isopure-protein-powder-review/'>Isopure review</a>, points to manufacturing under FDA good manufacturing practices and in-house quality assurance. Neither is the same as an independent program that screens finished lots for banned substances.
          </p>

          <p>
            For drug-tested athletes this settles it: neither powder should be your pick. Choose an isolate listed in the NSF or Informed Sport databases for the exact flavor you plan to buy. For everyone else, the gap is the same on both sides, so it does not tip the comparison.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our database has no published heavy-metal results for either powder, so we cannot compare them on lead or cadmium. That is a gap in what the brands publish, not a finding against either one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Which Has the Cleaner Label, Clean Simple Eats or Isopure?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Clean Simple Eats has the cleaner label by most buyers' definition: six ingredients, stevia as the only sweetener, no gums and no soy. Per the label, Brownie Batter lists grass-fed whey protein isolate, cocoa powder, natural flavors, sea salt, coconut milk powder and stevia leaf extract. Isopure Zero Carb lists whey protein isolate, a long vitamin and mineral blend, soy lecithin, natural and artificial flavor, salt, sucralose and xanthan gum.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sweetener is the clearest dividing line. Sucralose is approved and used in tiny amounts, but a lot of shoppers who land on Clean Simple Eats are deliberately avoiding it. If that is you, Isopure's flavored tubs are out, and only Isopure's Unflavored version or its clear protein, which uses stevia, would fit. Stevia has its own aftertaste that some people dislike, so this is a preference, not a health ranking.
          </p>

          <p>
            The vitamin blend is the less obvious difference. Every flavored Isopure tub we checked adds a vitamin and mineral blend, which Isopure markets as 20 vitamins and minerals. That is a plus if you skip a multivitamin, but a reason to pause if you already take one and drink two shakes a day. Clean Simple Eats adds no vitamins, but it does add a 100 mg enzyme blend, which the brand says aids digestion.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two details cut the other way. Clean Simple Eats contains coconut milk powder, so it is not the pick for anyone avoiding coconut, and its 'grass-fed' claim is the brand's own statement rather than a third-party certification. Isopure lists soy because of its lecithin. If you have either allergy, that single line decides the comparison before price or flavor.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="keto" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Which Is Better for Keto, Cutting and Sensitive Stomachs?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Isopure Zero Carb is the better fit for strict keto and hard cuts, and Clean Simple Eats is the better fit if gums or sucralose upset your stomach. Isopure's label shows 0 g of carbohydrate and 0 g of sugar, against 2 g of carbohydrate and under 1 g of sugar for Clean Simple Eats.
          </p>

          <p>
            In practice, 2 g of carbs per scoop will not break most keto plans, but on a 20 g daily limit two shakes of Clean Simple Eats use a fifth of it. Calories per gram of protein also favor Isopure: about 110 calories per 25 g of protein, against about 125 for Clean Simple Eats once you scale it up to the same protein. If you are cutting and every gram is tracked, Isopure is the leaner tool.
          </p>

          <p>
            For sensitive stomachs, both start from whey isolate, which has most of the lactose filtered out, so both are gentler than a concentrate blend. Clean Simple Eats adds an enzyme blend and leaves out gums. Isopure has no enzymes and adds xanthan gum, which some people find bloating in larger amounts. Neither brand publishes a lactose figure for these tubs, so very lactose-intolerant readers should start with a small amount.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your stomach is the main issue, our <a href='/best-whey-protein-isolate/'>best whey protein isolate</a> rankings include unflavored and low-additive isolates that remove sweeteners and gums entirely.
          </p>
        </div>

