            11. Which Tastes Better and Mixes Easier?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Clean Simple Eats has the bigger, more dessert-led flavor range, while Isopure keeps to a short list of classic flavors split across two tubs. We have not run our own taste panel on either, so this section reflects the flavor lists and buyer reviews, not hands-on testing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clean Simple Eats lists 12 flavors, including Brownie Batter, Simply Vanilla, Snickerdoodle, Cookies 'N Cream, Strawberry Cheesecake, Cake Batter, Mint Chocolate Cookie, Caramel Toffee, Toasted Marshmallow and an Unflavored option. The brand pitches its powder for baking and oats as well as shakes, and its recipe-led marketing shows in flavors built to taste like desserts.
          </p>

          <p>
            Isopure's split is the thing to know before ordering. The Zero Carb flavors listed on Vitacost are Creamy Vanilla, Strawberries and Cream, Banana Cream, Cookies and Cream and Vanilla Salted Caramel, with no plain chocolate. Chocolate lives in Isopure Low Carb, which adds cocoa and so carries 1 g of carbohydrate, alongside Dutch Chocolate, Chocolate Peanut Butter, Vanilla Bean, Toasted Coconut and Strawberry. If you want an Isopure chocolate shake, you are buying Low Carb, at about $2.39 per 25 g rather than $2.04.
          </p>

          <p>
            On mixing, the labels point to different textures. Isopure's lecithin helps the powder disperse in a shaker bottle, and its xanthan gum adds a little body and keeps the shake from separating. Clean Simple Eats leaves out both, so expect a thinner shake that may need a few extra seconds of shaking or a blender; its enzyme blend and coconut milk powder do not change that much. A small bag or tub is the cheapest way to check either flavor before committing.
          </p>
        </div>

