                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Clean Simple Eats contains coconut milk powder</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Baking with protein</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Clean Simple Eats</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Dessert flavors and an Unflavored option</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Wanting a clear, juice-style whey</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Isopure Clear</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $2.43 vs $3.44 per 25 g</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            If two rows pull in different directions, weigh how often you will use it. At one shake a day the price gap compounds to about $240 a year, which is the single largest practical difference. But a powder you dislike ends up half-finished in a cupboard, so if sucralose puts you off, Clean Simple Eats is the better buy for you even at the higher price.
          </p>

          <p>
            If neither fits, our <a href='/best-whey-protein-isolate/'>best whey protein isolate</a> guide ranks the wider field by Label Score and cost per 25 g, including certified options.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="skip-both" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            15. Who Should Skip Both?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Skip both if you are drug tested, have a milk allergy or eat vegan. Neither brand's whey has an NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Choice listing we could find, and both are dairy-based, so tested athletes need a certified isolate and dairy-free buyers need a plant protein.
          </p>

          <p>
            Also skip both if you want no sweetener at all and cannot find the unflavored versions in stock, or if you want the lowest possible cost per gram. Bulk unflavored isolates and some store brands undercut both on price. Our <a href='/protein-powder-comparison/'>protein powder comparison</a> lets you sort every product we have checked by cost per 25 g and filter by certification.
          </p>
        </div>

