        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            17. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Reading 20 g against 25 g as a like-for-like scoop</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Clean Simple Eats sizes its scoop at 100 calories and 20 g of protein, while Isopure's scoop gives 25 g. Two scoops a day of each are not the same dose. Compare cost and calories per 25 g of protein instead: about $2.71 and 125 calories for Clean Simple Eats against $2.04 and 110 for Isopure.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating a brand testing claim as certification</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Clean Simple Eats says its powders are third-party tested and Isopure cites in-house quality checks, but neither names an independent sport certifier we could verify. If you are drug tested, a brand statement is not enough. Look up the exact product and flavor in the NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport database before buying.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ordering Zero Carb and expecting chocolate</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Isopure's chocolate flavors sit in the Low Carb line, which adds cocoa, 1 g of carbohydrate and 210 mg of sodium, and costs about $2.39 per 25 g rather than $2.04. If you need a true zero-carb shake, stay with the vanilla, strawberry, banana or cookies flavors in the Zero Carb tub.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the smallest Clean Simple Eats bag for the price</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The 10-serving bag looks cheap at $29.99 but works out to about $3.75 per 25 g of protein, against $2.71 in the 30-serving bag. Trying a flavor in a small bag makes sense; buying small bags month after month roughly adds a third more to the cost of every shake.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Doubling up on vitamins without noticing</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Flavored Isopure tubs include an added vitamin and mineral blend, which Isopure markets as 20 vitamins and minerals. Two shakes a day on top of a multivitamin can stack up the same nutrients. Check the panel against your supplement, or choose Clean Simple Eats, which adds no vitamin blend.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            18. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Clean Simple Eats better than Isopure?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  For most buyers, no. Isopure Zero Carb gives 25 g of protein per scoop with 0 g of carbs, costs about $2.04 per 25 g against about $2.71 for Clean Simple Eats, and scores 63 to 48 on our Label Score. Clean Simple Eats is better if you want stevia instead of sucralose, no soy or gums, grass-fed whey and dessert flavors.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Isopure vs Clean Simple Eats: which has fewer calories?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Per scoop, Clean Simple Eats has fewer: 100 calories against 110 for Isopure Zero Carb, per the labels. Per gram of protein, Isopure is leaner, because its scoop holds 25 g of protein against 20 g. Scaled to 25 g of protein, Clean Simple Eats comes to about 125 calories and Isopure stays at 110.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Clean Simple Eats protein third-party tested?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  The brand says it is, for pathogens, heavy metals and gluten, but it names no certifier and we found no published lot certificates. We could not find an NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Choice listing at the time of writing. Isopure is in the same position, so drug-tested athletes should choose a certified isolate from another brand.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does Isopure use artificial sweeteners?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  The flavored Zero Carb and Low Carb tubs do. Both list sucralose and natural and artificial flavor, per the Vitacost labels we read. Isopure's clear protein uses stevia instead and says it has no artificial sweeteners or flavors. Clean Simple Eats Whey uses only stevia leaf extract, so it is the pick if you avoid sucralose.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Which is better for keto, Clean Simple Eats or Isopure?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Isopure Zero Carb, because its label shows 0 g of carbohydrate and 0 g of sugar per 25 g scoop. Clean Simple Eats has 2 g of carbohydrate per 20 g scoop. That small amount fits most keto plans, but on a strict 20 g daily limit two Clean Simple Eats shakes use about a fifth of the budget.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does Isopure or Clean Simple Eats contain soy or coconut?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Isopure Zero Carb and Low Carb list soy because they use soy lecithin. Clean Simple Eats Whey has no soy but contains coconut milk powder, though its allergen line declares only milk. Both contain milk. If you avoid soy, Clean Simple Eats fits; if you avoid coconut, Isopure fits. Always check the bag or tub you buy.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Which clear protein is better, Clean Simple Eats Clear or Isopure Clear?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Isopure Clear is the better value. It gives 20 g of protein for 90 calories and costs about $2.43 per 25 g in its 36-serving tub, against 100 calories and about $3.44 per 25 g for Clean Simple Eats Clear in the 20-serving bag. Both use stevia. Clean Simple Eats wins only if you prefer its candy-style flavors.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
        </div>

